{ bielmo():
    Jagna slipped out of the mist by the fence and stood before the Lord of the Forest, not lowering her eyes.
    “This man owes me the first thing out of the forest,” she said. # pokaz: jagna
    “What came out of the forest came out by my word,” the Lord of the Forest replied.
    “The word is yours, the debt is mine.” Jagna turned to Jaromir. “My grandmother bought and took upon herself what others were afraid to touch, and she paid for it with an eye. I collect after her what folk are owing.” She came so close that he caught the scent of St John’s wort on his face. “You came out of the forest too, Jaromir. You first, before ever you carried out {marysia_odzyskana:your daughter|that other one}.”
    She laid a finger on his left eyelid. It was dry and warm. When she drew it away, Jaromir’s left eye saw nothing but a milky mist, the same that sat in her own eye.
    “Now we’re quits,” she said, and went off into the mist.
}
{ cialo_kopii == oddane_jagnie:
    Jagna came out of the mist with something wrapped in linen and laid it at the feet of the Lord of the Forest. “I give back what isn’t mine,” she said. The cloth sank flat; beneath it there was nothing but dry leaves and bark. # pokaz: jagna
}
“I have come to settle our reckoning, to the very end,” said the Lord of the Forest. His voice rose from the ground, from the thatches, from the well. “The Oak, the word, the shrines. You have taken it all. My own came for your children because the fence I kept them behind has fallen. Tonight I have nothing to hold them back with.”
From behind him came a humming—the children under the oak had begun their tune. It passed from cottage to cottage, and in each one, behind bolted doors, some child woke and began to cry.{ marysia_odzyskana: Marysia woke too. She did not cry—she sat up on the bench and stared at the window, while Hanka held her by the shoulders.}
“You can give me back the boundary,” said the Lord of the Forest. “The old way: you no further than the boundary, and I—one child a generation. You can take my place—but you will never go back to your own.”
{ ekwipunek ? krzesiwo:
    He looked at Jaromir’s coat, and for the first time something like fear sounded in his voice. “Or you can do what you carry the flint and steel for. But after such a fire there will be neither forest nor any of you.”
- else:
    { zastaw_u_leszego == zastaw_krzesiwo:
        “You left the fire with me. That was wise.”
    }
}
