        The little one stood still for a moment, not knowing which way to go. Then she ran to her mother and touched her hand, just as she did every evening. At that, Grandfather Maciek held out his hand to her, and she took hold of it without fear. The dead of Zapadlina went along the road to Beech Ridge, each towards their own rest, not into the forest—and she went with them, never once looking back. The Candlemas candle in Hanka’s hand went out of its own accord, quietly, the way one snuffs a candle before sleep. # pokaz: marysia
    - else:
        That one, in the little white shift, paused a moment longer at the edge. “This one I took when there was no word,” said the Lord of the Forest, without looking round. “And she was with me too long.”{ ekwipunek ? wstazka_marysi: Jaromir went up to her, unwound the faded ribbon from his wrist and tied it in a bow in her hair, the way Hanka used to.} The little one turned and waved her small hand to her mother. Hanka raised her own. The candle in her other hand went out of its own accord, quietly, the way one snuffs a candle before sleep. # pokaz: marysia
    }
}
Over Zapadlina the light came—plainly, greyly, as on any November dawn. In the cottages the children stopped crying. # pokaz: swit # dalej
