    In the morning Hanka found the cap and understood everything. She kept it in the bosom of her dress. # dalej
-   Together they went to the edge of the forest. By the stump of the Old Oak the Lord of the Forest dwindled into an old man in a sheepskin turned fleece-out and a moss hat—just as Jaromir had seen him on the night of his wedding. # pokaz: starzec
He took off the hat and set it on Jaromir’s head. It was heavy and cold and smelled of wet bark. The moment he felt it, something inside Jaromir cracked softly—like ice on a pond—and he heard the forest. Every tree. Every root. Every creature settling down that very moment to sleep in its burrow before winter. And in that same instant he could not remember how Marysia’s hair had smelled. # pokaz: kapelusz
{ zastaw_u_leszego == zastaw_siekiera:
    The old man drew the grandfather’s axe out of the darkness, still tangled in rootlets, and leaned it against the stump. Jaromir did not pick it up.
}
Then the former Lord of the Forest lay down beneath the stump. He let out a long sigh, like a tree when the frost lets go of it, and began to sink into the earth. Moss grew over him, leaves covered him, until nothing remained but a hummock cloaked in grey lichen. # pokaz: pagorek
“Now you count,” something whispered from under the ground.
Jaromir turned to the retinue. They all stood before him, waiting. # dalej
“Go back,” he said. His voice passed through the treetops from the forest all the way to the lake. “Home. All of you.”
They went. The rusalki to the lake, the mamunas to their stones, the rye mother into the stubble, the licho into the mist. The herbwoman’s curse had bound the old one’s hands. The new one’s hands it did not know.
