# tlo: pozar_boru
Jaromir took out the flint and steel. It was small and blackened, and it remembered the hands of his grandfather, who had dug it out of the ashes of the herbwoman’s hut.
“No,” said the Lord of the Forest. This time his voice came not from everywhere, but from a single place. “Grandson of Maciek. No.” # pokaz: leszy
Jaromir {cena_caluna == cena_zdrowie:dragged himself, on legs he could not feel,|ran} to the edge of the young wood, where the grey lichen had already crept right up to the farmyard fences, and struck. Once. Twice. The third spark leapt into the dry moss—and it caught in an instant, and after it the spruces, and after the spruces the forest, the way everything burns on which not a drop of rain has fallen for years. # pokaz: ogien # dalej
There was a great roar, as though the earth had heaved a sigh. Heat blasted into Jaromir’s face; there was a smell of resin and singed hair. Out of the forest, towards the village, stampeded roe deer, wild boar, hares, blinded, heedless of the people. The retinue howled with a single voice. The rusalki rushed for the lake, the mamunas after them, and the licho blew away with the first smoke.{ los_figurki == figurka_w_skrzyni: In Jaromir’s cottage the chest beneath the bed split with a crack and smoke poured from its seams: the figurine that no stove had ever consumed was burning at last like any ordinary wood.} The children under the oak fled into the forest; from deep within it their humming could still be heard. It broke off halfway and never began again.
The Lord of the Forest stood in the flames and did not run. The bark on his face split open, his antlers blazed like dry brushwood on a Midsummer bonfire, and his eyes looked at Jaromir calmly, to the very end.
“All counted,” he said, one last time. And he came down with a crash, the way an old oak falls. # pokaz: runal
{ wilk_zywy():
    { wilk_przy_mnie():
        The white wolf stood by Jaromir to the end and never flinched, though the sparks scorched his coat. # pokaz: wilk
    - else:
        The white wolf leapt into the fire after his master. Antek howled as though it were he who was burning.{ obietnica_antkowi: No one got the wolf—Jaromir had promised Antek as much. The fire took him.}
    }
}
{ not marysia_odzyskana:
    Out of the smoke ran a little one in a white shift, straight into Hanka’s arms. Hanka clasped her—and in that very instant the Candlemas candle went out. In her arms there was nothing left but warm ashes.
}
{ zastaw_u_leszego == zastaw_cien:
    Jaromir’s shadow never came back to him. It burned beneath the oak along with all the rest.
}
The herbwoman’s curse had begun with fire, and with fire it ended. But the wind turned. The flames leapt the young wood{ cialo_kopii == pod_mlodnikiem:, passed over the little grave with its stick and red ribbon,} and swept on towards the village—to the barns, to the thatches, to the cottages where children were crying.
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Run to his own cottage, for Hanka{ marysia_odzyskana: and Marysia}.]
    ~ noc_pozaru = antek_pod_stodola
    Jaromir {cena_caluna == cena_zdrowie:dragged himself home on numb legs|ran home}, carried{ marysia_odzyskana: Marysia and} Hanka out to the meadow, and when he turned round, he saw Antek. Antek had gone on his own to the barn at Staś’s, which stood nearest the young wood, and set about hacking at its walls{ cena_caluna == cena_antek: left-handed}, to bring it down before the fire could cross by it to the rest of the village. He hacked away until the roof caught fire above his head. They shouted at him to get out. He only shouted back, “Look after Franek for me!”—and no one could tell which one he meant. # pokaz: stodola
    The barn came down with him inside it—and the flames halted at the fallen beams. # pokaz: stodola_runela
    Jadwisia sat in the ashes until morning, with little Franek on her lap. # dalej
*   [Beat out the flames along the farmyard fences alone.]
    ~ noc_pozaru = oslepl
    Jaromir threw himself at it alone—with his coat, with a wet sack, with his bare hands—and beat at the flames along the farmyard fences until people with buckets had poured every drop of water from the well over the thatches. # pokaz: gaszenie
    The fire did not get through. But in the morning Jaromir could no longer see the light, nor Hanka, who was weeping over him in the mud. The heat had burned out his eyes. # dalej
*   [Plough a firebreak together with the whole village.]
    ~ noc_pozaru = pol_wsi
    “Horses!” shouted Jaromir. The smith harnessed horses to the plough, and the men ploughed a black strip of earth in front of the fences while the women beat at the fire with wet sheets. # pokaz: przesieka
    They fought for every thatch one by one, and the fire took whatever they could not save in time. By dawn half of Zapadlina lay in ashes: barns, pigsties, four cottages. The people were alive. But winter found half the village without a roof over their heads. # dalej
-   When the sun rose, the forest was black as far as the eye could see. Not a single tree was left standing. Smoke drifted above the ruins. It was said afterwards that where the oak had stood in the heart of the forest, there lay only a mound of grey ash, covered by the first snow. # pokaz: popiol # dalej
