= epilog_granica
# tlo: epilog_dab
# ozdobnik
Half a century later, on All Souls’ Day, old Jaromir sat on the bench beneath the cross at the edge of the forest.{ cena_caluna == cena_zdrowie: His legs were wrapped in a sheepskin, as they had been for fifty years.}{ bielmo(): For half a century his left eye had seen only mist.} The wayside shrines stood along the woods once more—they had put them up the very first spring, all together, and ever since every generation had renewed them before Whitsun.
{ zoladz_zasadzony:
    Right beside the stump of the Old Oak grew a young oak, already taller than a thatched roof. Over half a century it had grown into the old wood and swallowed the axe; from the bark, at the height of a man’s knee, only the rusted blade jutted out, and no one had ever dared to touch it. # pokaz: dab
}
{ marysia_odzyskana:
    Around Jaromir sat Marysia’s children—three of them, freckled, with dimples in their cheeks—and her children’s children.{ cena_caluna == cena_umowa: Every year on Midsummer Night Marysia, greying now, carried a loaf to the flat stones. She never told anyone why.}
- else:
    Around Jaromir sat Antek’s grandchildren—Jaromir had not lived to see any of his own.
}
Hanka sat beside him with the youngest on her lap, peeling an apple for the child.
“Grandfather, tell us about the Lord of the Forest,” begged the youngest.
{ zastaw_u_leszego == zastaw_cien:
    Jaromir smiled. The children had long since stopped wondering that their grandfather cast no shadow. That was just how it was.
}
“Some seventy years before I first looked towards the forest,” he began, “my grandfather came down into Zapadlina with a bundle on his shoulder…”
{ zoladz_zasadzony:
    The forest behind him stood quiet. On Midsummer Night the children of Zapadlina lit the bonfires beneath the young oak, leapt over the flames and gathered last year’s acorns from under the tree, and in the morning every one of them was asleep in their own bed. For half a century not one had gone missing. {marysia_odzyskana:One of Marysia’s grandchildren|The youngest of Antek’s grandchildren} was clutching an acorn in one fist just now, listening open-mouthed. # pokaz: zoledzie # dalej
- else:
    The forest behind him stood quiet. Only now and then, on Midsummer Night, a child would vanish from one cottage or another—and come back after a few nights, smelling of resin and remembering nothing. The last to go missing for a few nights had been the Kowalczyks’ little girl. She was sitting closest now, listening open-mouthed. # dalej
}
-> koniec_opowiesci

= epilog_miejsce
# tlo: epilog_bor
# ozdobnik
Half a century later the children of Zapadlina went into the woods for mushrooms and bilberries and came home safe and sound, their baskets full to the brim. The old folk said the forest had long since stopped leading anyone astray. That the paths went wherever one meant to go.
{noc_ognia == ratowal_szymona:Beneath the young wood, beside the little birch cross old Staś had once driven into the ground there|By the wayside shrine at the edge of the forest} there was a grave with no body in it, and a cross of juniper wood.{ przysiega_ksiedzu: In the churchyard at Bukowiec the priest had refused him a place.}
{ pozegnanie == pozegnal_sie:
    Hanka carved the inscription herself, when she was already old: Jaromir. Beyond the fence.
- else:
    There was no inscription. Hanka hung his cap on the cross, and the wind never once tore it away. # pokaz: czapka
}
{ marysia_odzyskana:
    Marysia came there every All Souls’ with her own children. She set a bowl of porridge and a cup of water on the grave, and in the morning the bowl stood empty, and beside it lay a fresh, green oak leaf.{ cena_caluna == cena_umowa: And on Midsummer Night she still carried a loaf to the flat stones—to the end of her life.} # pokaz: miski
- else:
    Hanka came there every All Souls’ for as long as her strength lasted. She set two bowls on the grave—a large one and a small one—and in the morning both were empty. # pokaz: miski
}
Sometimes children gathering brushwood saw a tall old man in a moss hat at the edge of the forest. He stood without moving, and whenever one of the children waved to him, he nodded—slowly, gravely. # pokaz: starzec
{marysia_odzyskana:His great-granddaughter|The little girl from Antek’s house} once stopped where the trees began and, for the first time, looked towards the forest on her own. From the other side someone watched her for a long while, as if trying to recall where he knew that small face from. # pokaz: prawnuczka # dalej
-> koniec_opowiesci

= epilog_ogien
# tlo: epilog_wrzosy
# ozdobnik
Half a century later the hills beyond Zapadlina bore nothing but heather and juniper. The forest never grew back. Timber was carted in from far away, from beyond Bukowiec, and the cottages went short of firewood. The young left for town and did not return. Of Zapadlina three cottages remained; of the rest—mounds overgrown with nettles.
{ noc_pozaru:
- antek_pod_stodola:
    In the churchyard at Bukowiec, beneath a birch, lay Antek’s grave. Franek, grey-haired now, swept it every Saturday.
- oslepl:
    For fifty years Jaromir had seen nothing. He was left his hearing—and the wind, which no longer had anything to whisper through.
- pol_wsi:
    The cottages raised after the fire were built of brick, carted in from town. People said brick does not burn.
}
{ cena_caluna == cena_zdrowie:
    Jaromir sat with his legs wrapped in a sheepskin—since that All Souls’ nothing had ever warmed them.
}
There was no curse. No one vanished on Midsummer Night.{ cena_caluna == cena_umowa: No one heard washing paddles by the lake—no one but Marysia, who every year carried a loaf to the flat stones.} On All Souls’ Day porridge and water stood on the windowsills, and in the morning they were still untouched.
Only once, in spring, {marysia_odzyskana:his granddaughter|Antek’s granddaughter} brought something down from the hill in her fist and showed it to Jaromir on her open palm. It was a tiny green spruce seedling, soft and warm as a fledgling. # pokaz: kielek
“Shall I throw it away?” she asked. “And what was it like, Grandfather, when there was a forest?”
Jaromir held the seedling between his fingers.{ noc_pozaru == oslepl: He could not see it, but he could feel it.} Then he gave it back to her.
“Plant it,” he said. “Far from the village.” He settled himself more comfortably. “Some seventy years before I first looked towards the forest that is no more…” # dalej
-> koniec_opowiesci

